{"causes":["The ongoing decline of the Western Roman Empire necessitated alliances with barbarian federates to bolster its depleted military forces.","Emperor Majorian's ambitious campaigns to restore Roman authority required skilled military commanders, regardless of their ethnic origin.","The presence of various barbarian groups, including Huns, within Roman territories created opportunities for individuals like Tuldila to achieve military prominence."],"effects":["Tuldila's leadership contributed to a temporary Roman victory against Vandal raiders in Campania in 458 CE.","His execution by Majorian underscored the deep-seated Roman distrust of powerful barbarian federates, despite their military utility.","The episode contributed to the broader narrative of Roman reliance on, and eventual subjugation by, barbarian forces in the 5th century."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"majorian","sourceName":"Majorian","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"tuldila","targetName":"Tuldila","context":"Majorian enlisted Tuldila as a Hunnic commander in his Roman army during his campaigns to restore imperial authority in the mid-5th century CE."},{"sourceSlug":"tuldila","sourceName":"Tuldila","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"vandal-raids-on-italy","targetName":"Vandal Raids on Italy","context":"Tuldila commanded Hunnic troops within Majorian's army that repelled a Vandal raiding party in Campania in 458 CE."},{"sourceSlug":"tuldila","sourceName":"Tuldila","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"campania-italy","targetName":"Campania, Italy","context":"Tuldila's military actions and subsequent execution took place in the region of Campania, Italy, in 458 CE."},{"sourceSlug":"hunnic-confederation","sourceName":"Hunnic Confederation","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"tuldila","targetName":"Tuldila","context":"Tuldila was a Hunnic chieftain, indicating his origin from the broader Hunnic confederation that had a significant impact on Europe."},{"sourceSlug":"tuldila","sourceName":"Tuldila","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"late-roman-military-strategy","targetName":"Late Roman Military Strategy","context":"Tuldila's service exemplifies the Roman Empire's strategy of incorporating barbarian federates into its military to combat external threats and maintain internal control."}],"places":[{"name":"Campania, Italy","role":"Site of victory and death"},{"name":"Sinuessa, Italy","role":"Battle location"},{"name":"Western Roman Empire","role":"Area of operation"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Tuldila","type":"Reference article","slug":"tuldila"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3621998","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuldila"],"tags":["Monarchs & Rulers","political-systems","empire-and-colonialism","geopolitical-linkage"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Tuldila (died 458 CE) was a king or military leader of the Suebi — the Germanic people who had established a kingdom in northwestern Iberia (modern Galicia and northern Portugal) following the Visigothic-led Germanic crossing of the Rhine in 406 CE and the subsequent Suebian settlement of Gallaecia in 409 CE. The Suebian kingdom of Gallaecia (c. 409–585 CE) was one of the first post-Roman successor kingdoms in western Europe, and Tuldila's record represents the turbulent internal politics and military conflicts of the Suebian state in its mid-5th-century phase.

Tuldila is documented primarily in the Chronicle of Hydatius — the mid-5th century bishop of Chaves (in modern Portugal) whose chronicle is the primary source for the history of northwestern Iberia from c. 379–468 CE and one of the most important sources for the transitional period from Roman to Germanic rule in the western provinces. Hydatius records Tuldila's death in 458 CE in the context of the Suebian kingdom's internal factional struggles and its conflicts with the Visigothic kingdom (which had conquered most of Iberia by the mid-5th century but had not yet fully subdued the Suebi of the northwest). The Suebian kingdom in this period was characterised by frequent internal dynastic conflicts, Christian controversies (between Catholic and Arian factions), and military confrontations with Visigothic pressure from the south and east.

Tuldila's specific role in Suebian politics — whether he was a king, a sub-king, a military commander, or a noble — is uncertain due to the brief and elliptical nature of Hydatius's references. He represents the type of documented but poorly understood figure from the 'Migration Period' whose name survives in the fragmentary chronicles of the post-Roman world without the context that would allow his specific significance to be fully recovered.","subjects":["Migration Period","Suebian History","Classical Era","Iberian History","Germanic Peoples","Ancient History","Post-Roman Europe","Galicia"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":6,"significanceNarrative":"Tuldila's brief career as a Hunnic commander in Majorian's army illustrates the complex and often fatal alliances between the declining Western Roman Empire and barbarian federates, contributing to the narrative of Roman military adaptation and eventual collapse. His actions highlight the precarious balance of power and distrust that characterized late Roman-barbarian relations.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
